==============================================================================
M46 b3 -- BLUR-ONLY vs SMOOTH-ONLY, the separation test
==============================================================================

   rho_fpn = 0.3094 (from b1, not chosen).  8 noise draws per point.
   S_null is a CONSTANT latent: no detail by construction, unlike the
   observed flat ROI whose rule-10 detector b2 measured at 6.43.

1. THE INFEASIBLE MATCHING DIRECTION, reported not deleted
   Can a noise reduction imitate a blur?  Solve k so that
   hp_sd(SMOOTH) == hp_sd(BLUR) on S_detail:
   sb=2.000  -> NO SOLUTION (hp_sd target 27.4937; residual with ALL noise removed +31.1960)
   sb=1.000  -> NO SOLUTION (hp_sd target 44.4940; residual with ALL noise removed +14.1957)
   sb=0.500  -> NO SOLUTION (hp_sd target 54.0292; residual with ALL noise removed +4.6605)
   sb=0.250  -> NO SOLUTION (hp_sd target 57.4566; residual with ALL noise removed +1.2331)
   sb=0.125  -> NO SOLUTION (hp_sd target 58.4523; residual with ALL noise removed +0.2374)
   MEASURED: none.  The merged detail ROI carries hp_sd 58.8 DN of
   scene against sigma 3.69 DN of noise, so deleting ALL the
   noise costs less than a 0.125 px blur.  On this corpus a merge
   cannot fake sharpness by denoising -- which is a fact about the
   DATA, not about any metric.

2. THE FEASIBLE MATCHING.  Fix k = sqrt(2) = 1.4142 -- the noise a
   DOUBLING of the acceptance set buys, i.e. a real merge change --
   and solve for the blur that costs the same hp_sd on S_detail.
   sb = 0.04999 px   (hp_sd target 58.7452)
   hp_sd's separation score is therefore 0.500 BY CONSTRUCTION on
   S_detail.  Every other statistic is now asked whether it can do
   better on the SAME pair of plants.

